From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: B Honey <9922368+Mhsbrian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ANYBOOK: abi: allow filp_open, kernel_read and kernel_write
 on partner lists

NVIDIA's open kernel modules (ANAL-297) are built as AnyBook vendor modules
for the G14's RTX 5070 Ti Laptop GPU. Their runtime D3 (RTD3) keeps the GPU's
PCI sysfs config file open with filp_open() and reads it to drop the device's
runtime PM reference; with suspend notifiers they save video memory to an
unlinked shmem file with kernel_write() and restore it with kernel_read().
NVIDIA's Android build mode (PLATFORM_IS_ANDROID=1) avoids all three, but then
the dGPU never runtime-suspends and only some video memory survives suspend,
which defeats the battery work the driver is for.

So the AnyBook kernel takes an explicit exception to the GKI denylist for
these three symbols, for the AnyBook partner list. kernel_read_file and
kernel_read_file_from_path stay denied. The modules import filp_open from the
ANDROID_GKI_VFS namespace explicitly.

This changes which symbols a list may name, not how any symbol is exported:
the kernel still exports exactly what its lists name.

Change-Id: Icaab34d594554d11f591d14223fb72bd85e142b4
---
 abi/symbols.deny | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/abi/symbols.deny b/abi/symbols.deny
index bd4a881d..8a0cc92c 100644
--- a/abi/symbols.deny
+++ b/abi/symbols.deny
@@ -17,11 +17,15 @@
 # File access symbols that are forbidden because drivers should never
 # try to access files directly.  Instead proper user/kernel apis should
 # always be used.
-filp_open					Drivers should not open files directly
-kernel_read					Drivers should never read from a file
+#
+# AnyBook (ANAL-297): filp_open, kernel_read and kernel_write are allowed on
+# the AnyBook partner list (gki/x86_64/symbols/anybook_amd) for NVIDIA's open
+# kernel modules alone. Their runtime D3 keeps the GPU's PCI sysfs config file
+# open (filp_open), and they save and restore video memory across suspend in an
+# unlinked shmem file (kernel_write, kernel_read). NVIDIA's Android build mode
+# (PLATFORM_IS_ANDROID=1) avoids these calls, but also loses both features.
 kernel_read_file			Drivers should never read from a file
 kernel_read_file_from_path	Drivers should never read from a file
-kernel_write				Drivers should never write to a file
 
 # Kprobe needs to be left alone
 disable_kprobe		kprobe should not be touched by any driver
